#X text -224 434 This function Maps pitch/roll angle to the interval between drum samples. The interval decreases rapidly at small angles \, then gradually levels off as it approaches 1000 ms., f 29;
#X text 466 1408 As with the version of the system that uses Direct Mappings \, Yaw is split into sections. This ensures that only one drum voice is evolving at a time. For example \, if Yaw is at 180 degrees \, only the snare voice is being controlled by the user., f 62;
#X text -104 175 This counter loop sends IMU data to a new array index every time data is received. Once index 200 has been reached \, the counter resets back to 0 and old data is overwritten. Essentially a circular buffer., f 19;
#X text 560 153 Defining and sizing arrays for gesture vector creation. Note that Yaw is not used \, but is left in there incase of future redesign., f 66;
#X text 232 375 This logic is to ensure the metronome only starts when data is received., f 22;
#X text 217 555 Statistical analysis on circular buffers is performed every 100ms. This calculates the average Pitch/Roll and difference in Pitch/Roll over 200 data points \, as well as the maximum linear acceleration magnitude., f 87;
#X text 346 847 Note that the average linear acceleration magnitude is available but is not currently being used., f 29;
#X text 707 1284 Reads regressor JSON file with learned mapping information.;
#X text 510 1203 Gestural feature vector is normalised.;
#X text 645 1335 Regressor predicts the normalised output - delta X and delta Y to increase/decrease current corpus coordinates., f 62;
#X text 405 1081 Reads learned input extrema JSON file for normalising the feature vector so it can be interpreted by the regressor., f 32;
#X text -287 1283 Reads learned ouput extrema JSON file for denormalising the regressor output., f 32;
#X text -84 1404 Output vector is denormalised to give final output delta X and delta Y., f 30;
